        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
State-of-the-art single phase active thermal control systems (ATCSs) consume too much power to be used as spacecraft grow in size and electric power. Two-phase systems are attractive because they use the liquid/vapor phase change energy to transport heat rather than the sensible energy of single phase fluid temperature change. This decreases the required overall mass flow rate with a concomitant decrease in the pumping power requirement. The two-phase ATCSs developed to date have suffered from relatively high power consumption, which would not allow the two-phase power gains to be fully realized on future space vehicles. A low power two-phase ATCS (LPTPA) has been under development at NASA Johnson Space Center (JSC) for the last several years. The system differs from prior designs by not performing phase separation and using mature, efficient centrifugal pump technology. This allows a power savings of 50% over established two-phase system designs. By significantly decreasing the power requirement, the LPTPA will allow significant savings in system power on future vehicles (up to 80% over state-of-the-art single phase systems). The purpose of the proposed work was to increase the LPTPA TRL from 3 to 5. A prototypic LPTPA has been analyzed, designed, and was recently assembled at JSC. The prototypic LPTPA uses R134a as the working fluid, is scaled to approximate an International Space Station (ISS) sized loop, and has plumbing that minimizes gravitational effects. During the proposed effort, the LPTPA was tested, refined, and retested. The first phase ran the LPTPA in manual control over a wide range of heat loads and operating conditions. This will prove the basic concept, demonstrate system stability, and provide information for control automation. Second, the LPTPA was tested in automatic control mode, incorporating any refinements that are identified in the first phase of testing. The expected outcome of the project is a prototypic system whose stable operation has been demonstrated under automated control over a wide range of heat loads and operating conditions.
